A: The problem seems to be the way you've set up your PexObserve.ValueForViewing calls. Currently, you are displaying old and new values, but not any indication on which property you're observing. 
You should name it clearly to what being observed (like adding to the list or removing from the list), which property is it (count, add return value, etc.), what are the old and new values for that property. 
It's also better to use PexDataForViewing instead of PexObserve.ValueForViewing if you're going to display values, but you still need to provide meaningful names for your fields. 
You might also want to consider refining your Pex settings if you're getting results that are unexpected, such as getting a new value to be zero after inserting an item, or sometimes it doesn't produce any valid values at all. 
Lastly, make sure the ArrayList is being tested correctly by ensuring it's testing with all its valid states and edge cases. Since you're not providing a clear test function here, I suggest considering using static example inputs for all methods being tested. 
Also, make sure to test the methods being observed in a controlled environment (without affecting the rest of your system, it's just for tests). 
I hope this helps and good luck with your testing.

A: To help PEx understand the behavior of your ArrayList (or any other complex object), you can utilize DataAccessors. 

DataAccessors are special types of methods that Pex can invoke and analyze. They must be public and, in .NET, stateless. 

It is important to remember you need to understand the data structure and operations you are using. Let's consider an example of your PUT_AddContract method to include Data Accessors.

[PexMethod, PexExploration] //Added PexExploration attribute
public void PUT_AddContract([PexAssume--------------------        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);
 
            int New_Ret = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

        }
    }
}

